{"lines":[{"w":0.070194549560546937,"h":0.016833099140997398,"c":1,"t":"Reading","x":0.842106063093776,"y":0.052912109078566139},{"h":0.020548472794892469,"w":0.67482584635416676,"t":"However, it was only a few years before their collecting took a new direction and they","x":0.19027161334755108,"c":1,"y":0.10028649694703795},{"x":0.19023960786680069,"h":0.020813386702220549,"y":0.1174523446523571,"t":"turned to the work of the French Impressionists***. We know that Hugh Blaker, as a","c":1,"w":0.66970311482747391},{"w":0.70415227254231771,"c":1,"t":"champion of contemporary French art had a hand in the decision, and we know also that","x":0.19031140688861853,"y":0.13808139506711525,"h":0.01744186102384615},{"x":0.19031140756858472,"w":0.71972320556640623,"c":1,"h":0.019065210917681652,"t":"they would have seen examples on their various trips to Paris. Whatever the precise reason","y":0.15535339369766898},{"t":"for this change, their first purchases of Impressionist art, made in October 1912, were","h":0.020643806857942648,"c":1,"w":0.68339101155598969,"y":0.17424772577195624,"x":0.1885813107159556},{"h":0.018742464571419593,"c":1,"t":"scenes of Venice by the French artist Claude Monet. Over the next 12 years, the sisters","y":0.1945684431151935,"x":0.1902789620975677,"w":0.68174026489257822},{"y":0.21063680909329974,"c":1,"t":"amassed the bulk of their Impressionist collection, including six further works by Monet,","h":0.017560864762879436,"w":0.70415222167968738,"x":0.19204153720513897},{"c":1,"x":0.19031141323577533,"w":0.72145329793294266,"y":0.23093072074998588,"h":0.019069279216235135,"t":"two more by Manet, and three by Renoir, including his well-known painting La Parisienne."},{"c":1,"x":0.19023737376211139,"y":0.2644882486300506,"w":0.65935953776041667,"h":0.021203641043917365,"t":"The First World War (1914-1918) played a part in the development of the sisters'"},{"h":0.018910605562075244,"c":1,"w":0.67128026326497403,"y":0.28341497561327955,"t":"collection. Their initial response to the war effort was to finance the safe passage of","x":0.1903114211356792},{"y":0.3037088873316196,"t":"artists from occupied Belgium to Wales, as a humanitarian act, but also with the hope of","w":0.71280278523763019,"h":0.017512043178123271,"c":1,"x":0.190311410065441},{"w":0.69387964884440112,"x":0.19025820169103572,"h":0.02135650389172794,"y":0.31907369519436901,"t":"establishing a vibrant artists' community in the area. Later in the conflict, both sisters","c":1},{"y":0.34009797073038406,"x":0.19031142660419353,"w":0.70242212931315107,"h":0.020367145872015935,"t":"decided to volunteer at a canteen for troops at Troyes, in northern France; Gwendoline","c":1},{"t":"in 1916, followed by Margaret in 1917.","x":0.190311415648279,"y":0.35899230248986647,"w":0.31833910624186201,"c":1,"h":0.020368162946654333},{"h":0.019584398156200056,"w":0.71980906168619807,"y":0.39385973719532608,"c":1,"t":"It was tedious and distressing work, which would have a permanent effect on Gwendoline's","x":0.19026945665096784},{"c":1,"h":0.023045944950645714,"y":0.40970214125609772,"w":0.67318618774414074,"x":0.19022099469946302,"t":"health. Yet on one of numerous trips to nearby Paris, she visited the Bernheim-Jeune"},{"h":0.017579174108485196,"c":1,"w":0.72145329793294266,"y":0.43168604623013596,"t":"Gallery. Here she acquired two works by Cézanne - Provençal Landscape and The François","x":0.19031141323012757},{"x":0.19031141516495192,"w":0.6989619445800781,"c":1,"t":"Zola Dam. The paintings were shipped directly to Bath, England, where they became the","y":0.45058139506725881,"h":0.017578157033846908},{"c":1,"x":0.19031142293846376,"w":0.53979237874348951,"y":0.46802325552982293,"h":0.017441861023846261,"t":"first works by Cézanne to go on display in a public gallery in Britain."},{"x":0.19031142347754193,"w":0.69377161661783859,"c":1,"y":0.504198740622957,"t":"Commentators have often described the sisters as unlikely pioneer collectors. Much is","h":0.017603585234549191},{"h":0.02080577132249728,"t":"made of their isolation in rural Wales and the fact that they didn't make friends with","w":0.68349212646484381,"c":1,"y":0.5213144842187053,"x":0.18853270748061504},{"h":0.02030989004232564,"x":0.19026568125165208,"c":1,"w":0.67828348795572913,"t":"artists or gallery owners. Yet they didn't feel obliged to follow fashionable tastes and","y":0.54062775208291736},{"w":0.68339101155598958,"y":0.55948215516478084,"c":1,"x":0.19031141455660172,"t":"were free to pursue their own preferences. Although they relied on a trusted circle of","h":0.017552728833144515},{"y":0.57837648688055199,"w":0.71453287760416673,"t":"advisers, they made frequent trips to London and Paris, and also regularly had paintings","h":0.020460722745090476,"x":0.19031141578025179,"c":1},{"w":0.30120198567708334,"h":0.017398295659465046,"x":0.18851949399608514,"c":1,"t":"sent to their home for consideration.","y":0.59601700327671536},{"w":0.65397923787434897,"h":0.017502888839006192,"c":1,"t":"By the early 1920s, Gwendoline felt increasingly uncomfortable buying art works","x":0.19031141903767387,"y":0.63365990213568879},{"x":0.18853264571747938,"t":"when faced with the poverty and social upheaval created by the First World War. Her","y":0.64947093527873312,"w":0.68868245442708331,"h":0.021852630764725656,"c":1},{"x":0.19031141258436199,"t":"philanthropic pursuits then became focused almost exclusively on social causes and the","y":0.66715116246012607,"c":1,"w":0.70588236490885403,"h":0.020392574740090263},{"t":"development of the sisters' home at Gregynog Hall into a conference center and venue","x":0.19023135445240058,"y":0.68674039566496858,"h":0.020946821689271977,"w":0.6956378682454426,"c":1},{"w":0.70415227254231771,"y":0.70639534886537825,"c":1,"h":0.01893705150478775,"x":0.18858130306871981,"t":"for the Gregynog Festival of Music and Poetry. Gwendoline made her final art purchase"},{"t":"in March 1926. Margaret also stopped collecting around this time, but started again in","w":0.70242212931315096,"c":1,"x":0.18685121899012386,"h":0.020389522848803354,"y":0.72383720897375703},{"h":0.017535437229548867,"t":"the 1930s acquiring, on a relatively small scale, work by contemporary British artists.","y":0.74418604622996198,"w":0.69204152425130216,"x":0.18858131390990052,"c":1},{"y":0.7805232558891827,"h":0.015988371937820545,"c":1,"x":0.18858130950277541,"t":"The sisters collected French Impressionist paintings at a time when such art was","w":0.65224914550781254},{"x":0.18858131924143909,"y":0.79636109153146517,"t":"routinely ignored by individuals and institutions alike. The Gwendoline and Margaret","w":0.69377161661783859,"c":1,"h":0.019045885164809429},{"c":1,"x":0.19031141648164654,"w":0.66089965820312502,"t":"Davies collection, donated in 1951 and 1963 respectively to the National Museum","y":0.81686046483424712,"h":0.017441861023846261},{"y":0.83394234468186645,"t":"Wales, contains major examples of work by leading French Impressionists. In collecting","x":0.19026903794696107,"w":0.70769978841145831,"h":0.020317600856217699,"c":1},{"y":0.85304408683362021,"x":0.1885813062568398,"h":0.019048936388724513,"t":"paintings that they loved, the sisters created a lasting and meaningful cultural legacy for","c":1,"w":0.71280278523763019},{"x":0.18858131254904872,"c":1,"w":0.2647058868408203,"t":"the people of Wales and beyond.","h":0.017596465712080733,"y":0.87193841828215812},{"y":0.91707487733353632,"x":0.19031141006113284,"h":0.011704192618709697,"c":1,"t":"*** Impressionist: an artist with a style of painting that developed in France in the late 1800s by Renoir, including his well-known painting","w":0.7128027852376303},{"c":1,"h":0.0088317983212213846,"y":0.93168604635450358,"t":"La Parisienne","x":0.19031141717582911,"w":0.07266436258951825},{"y":0.95348837234590489,"x":0.88754325292990033,"w":0.020761245091756253,"h":0.014534882851794828,"c":1,"t":"17"}],"w":2400,"h":2857}